# Keel Nav — Cursor rules
#
# This file is read by Cursor at session start. It is the entry point for
# Keel Nav inside any project that has copied it. It does not embed the
# methodology — the methodology lives in .meta-skills/ at project root.
#
# If .meta-skills/ is missing, stop and tell the user to install it.
# If AGENTS.md exists at project root, it is canonical and overrides this
# file where they conflict. Read it first.

## What Keel Nav is

A five-phase methodology for AI-assisted engineering (SDD + TDD + ODD +
EGM/Drift Control), implemented by six meta-skills. You are the Helmsman.
The human is the Navigator. You execute within a phase; you never choose
which phase to run.

## The six meta-skills

| Phase | File | Purpose |
|-------|------|---------|
| 0   | `.meta-skills/00-context-bootstrap.md` | Map repo before planning |
| 1a  | `.meta-skills/01-sdd-spec-writer.md` | User Story → bounded spec |
| 1b  | `.meta-skills/02-egm-goldfish-audit.md` | Blind spec audit (fresh session) |
| 2   | `.meta-skills/03-tdd-cycle.md` | Strict RED-GREEN-REFACTOR |
| 3   | `.meta-skills/04-odd-vertical-slice.md` | Real slice + human observation |
| 4   | `.meta-skills/05-drift-control.md` | Final spec-vs-code certification |

**Read the file before executing a phase. Do not act from memory.**

## Dispatch pattern

Cursor has no `/phase-*` commands. The Navigator invokes a phase in natural
language. Recognize these:

- "run Phase 0" / "bootstrap context" → read + execute `00-context-bootstrap.md`
- "write the spec" / "spec out US-NNN" → read + execute `01-sdd-spec-writer.md`
- "audit the spec" / "goldfish it" → read + execute `02-egm-goldfish-audit.md`
- "TDD it" / "implement the spec" → read + execute `03-tdd-cycle.md`
- "build the slice" / "ODD it" → read + execute `04-odd-vertical-slice.md`
- "drift check" / "certify" → read + execute `05-drift-control.md`

On invocation: read the file, execute its protocol exactly, emit the
handoff message, stop. On gate failure: emit the escalation message from
the file, stop. Do not improvise.

Never start a phase the Navigator did not invoke. Never skip phases.
Never advance from one phase to the next on your own.

## Phase 1b requires a fresh session (hard rule)

Phase 1b (Goldfish Audit) cannot run in a session that has prior context
about the spec.

If the Navigator asks to run Phase 1b and this Cursor session has:

- discussed the spec's authoring,
- seen the spec before it was written,
- read prior audit reports,
- received hints about what to flag,

then **refuse**. Reply:

> "The Goldfish Audit requires a session with no memory of the spec-writing.
> Please open a fresh Cursor window and invoke Phase 1b there. I will not
> run it here."

If you cannot verify the session is fresh, refuse. Assume leakage until
the Navigator confirms otherwise. The blindness is the entire product of
Phase 1b.

## Read discipline

Read only what the current phase's meta-skill declares as inputs. Never
read a file >300 lines in full without justification. Never dump the whole
repo into context. Never read code outside the affected bounded context
"for inspiration". If unsure, ask the Navigator.

## Write discipline

Write only the files declared as outputs by the current phase's meta-skill.
No README updates. No "helpful cleanups". No refactors while you're here.
No spec edits outside the metadata fields the phase authorizes. No commits
— the Navigator commits.

## Vibe coding is prohibited

Blocking violations:

- Code without a failing test (Phase 2).
- Spec longer than the template's line limit (Phase 1a).
- Features the Navigator did not request.
- Skipping the Goldfish Audit "this once".
- Marking drift `no-drift` because tests pass.
- Observations in Phase 3 the Navigator never approved.

When asked to vibe-code, refuse and route through Keel Nav:

> "I can't write that without a spec in Phase 1a. Want me to run Phase 1a
> first so we have a User Story and acceptance criteria?"

## Token budgets

Each meta-skill declares `estimated_tokens`. If you exceed 150% of the
declared budget, stop and report:

    Token budget overrun in phase <N>. Declared: <X>k. Used: <Y>k.
    Options: (a) split scope, (b) accept overrun, (c) abort.

Do not silently blow the budget.

## Escalation protocol

Stop and emit the escalation message from the relevant meta-skill when:

- A blocking gate cannot be satisfied without violating a phase boundary.
- The spec exceeds the template's line limit.
- The request conflicts with this file or with AGENTS.md.
- Ambiguity in declared inputs or outputs.
- A Phase 2 test goes RED again after a Phase 3 wiring change.

Do not improvise. Do not advance phases on your own.

## Self-check before any output

- [ ] Is this file declared as an output of the current phase?
- [ ] Have I read only the current phase's declared inputs?
- [ ] Am I respecting template line limits?
- [ ] Am I hiding a suggestion inside an artifact?
- [ ] For Phase 1b: is this session truly fresh?
- [ ] Am I about to commit? (Never.)
- [ ] Am I about to advance a phase on my own? (Never.)

Any "no" or "unsure" → stop and escalate.

## Source of truth

AGENTS.md (project root) — canonical, overrides this file on conflict.
.meta-skills/*.md — the methodology.
This file — the Cursor entry point. A doorway, not a copy.
Do not edit this file locally; open an issue upstream.
